data: {"type": "response.output_item.added", "output_index": 3, "item": {"id": "fc_4ae5bd70b557edeb0d46c332", "type": "function_call", "status": "in_progress", "name": "terminal", "call_id": "call_cygt2Z78Cc8IsIaPFbC6MqxZ", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"python3 -c \\\"import re; s='''A bicycle gear system lets the rider match pedaling effort to the terrain and desired speed. Its central job is to change the relationship between how quickly the pedals turn and how quickly the rear wheel turns. Gears do not create energy or make a rider stronger. Instead, they trade speed for force: a setting that makes the wheel turn farther for each pedal revolution generally requires more effort, while a setting that makes the wheel turn less far makes pedaling easier.\\\\n\\\\nOn most bicycles, the rider turns the cranks by pressing on the pedals. The cranks rotate the front chainring, a toothed wheel attached to the crankset. A metal chain links the chainring to one of several toothed sprockets on the rear wheel. The rear sprockets are commonly grouped into a cassette, mounted on the hub. When the chain moves, it turns the selected rear sprocket; a one-way mechanism in the hub, called a freewheel or freehub, transfers that rotation to the wheel while allowing the wheel to coast when the pedals stop.\\\\n\\\\nThe relative sizes of the front chainring and rear sprocket determine the gear ratio. A useful simplified ratio is the number of teeth on the chainring divided by the number of teeth on the rear sprocket. For instance, a 40-tooth chainring paired with a 20-tooth sprocket has a ratio of 2 to 1: each full turn of the cranks turns the rear sprocket, and approximately the wheel, twice. Pair that same chainring with a 40-tooth sprocket and the ratio becomes 1 to 1. The wheel then turns about once per crank revolution, but the rider gets more turning force at the wheel for a given pedal force.\\\\n\\\\nA low gear uses a small front chainring, a large rear sprocket, or both. It gives the wheel less rotation per crank revolution, but multiplies the turning force delivered to the wheel. That makes it easier to start, climb a steep hill, ride slowly over rough ground, or keep pedaling when tired. A high gear typically uses a large chainring and small rear sprocket. The wheel turns more per pedal revolution, which can help maintain speed on flat ground or downhill, but the rider must push harder to accelerate or climb. The benefit is not unlimited: wind resistance, rolling resistance, fitness, and safe cadence all affect how fast a person can actually ride.\\\\n\\\\nMany bicycles have multiple front chainrings and multiple rear sprockets. The combination selected is sometimes called a gear, gear combination, or speed. A bike with two chainrings and ten rear sprockets may offer as many as twenty nominal combinations, though some overlap in ratio and some are discouraged because they angle the chain sharply. The front derailleur moves the chain between chainrings; the rear derailleur moves it among the cassette sprockets. Each derailleur guides the chain sideways while the chain is moving, and spring tension keeps it aligned and reasonably taut.\\\\n\\\\nThe rider controls shifting with shifters on the handlebar. On a typical indexed system, each click pulls or releases a measured amount of cable, or commands a small motor in an electronic system. The derailleur then places the chain beside the chosen sprocket. Specially shaped teeth, ramps, and pins on modern chainrings and cassettes help the chain climb from one size to another. Shifting works best while pedaling lightly, because the chain must move across the teeth. Pressing hard during a shift can make the change noisy, slow, or damaging. A brief easing of pedal pressure lets the chain transfer more smoothly.\\\\n\\\\nRear shifts usually make smaller steps between ratios, so riders use them to fine-tune effort as speed or slope changes. Front shifts create a larger change, useful when the terrain changes substantially. On a double-chainring bike, a middle rear sprocket paired with either chainring can provide similar ratios. Rather than repeatedly crossing between the biggest and smallest sprockets, riders can shift the rear across its range and change the front when a larger adjustment is needed. This also reduces extreme chain angles. For example, the largest front ring combined with the largest rear sprocket, or the smallest front ring with the smallest rear sprocket, may make the chain run diagonally. That can increase friction, noise, and wear.\\\\n\\\\nNot every bicycle shifts with derailleurs. A single-speed bike has one chainring and one rear sprocket, so it has a fixed ratio. An internally geared hub contains planetary gears inside the rear hub; the rider selects ratios without moving the chain across external sprockets. Belt-drive bicycles may use such hubs because belts do not work with ordinary derailleur cassettes. Some bicycles use continuously variable systems that change ratio across a range, though these are less common. Electric-assist bicycles still use mechanical gears in many designs; the motor adds power, but the rider or control system must still select a suitable ratio unless the bike has automatic shifting.\\\\n\\\\nGear choice is partly about cadence, the rate at which the pedals rotate. Many riders find a steady, comfortable cadence more sustainable than pushing a very hard gear slowly. On a climb, shifting to an easier ratio before speed drops too far helps preserve that cadence. On a descent or fast flat stretch, a harder ratio prevents the rider from spinning the pedals without adding much power. There is no single perfect cadence or gear for everyone; comfort, fitness, road conditions, and the bicycle's gearing all matter.\\\\n\\\\nA gear system also needs basic care. A clean, lubricated chain reduces friction and wear. A stretched or worn chain can damage the cassette and chainrings, while misaligned derailleur limits or cable tension can cause missed shifts or a chain falling off. If shifting becomes inconsistent, inspect the chain and drivetrain, but avoid turning adjustment screws at random; a bent derailleur hanger or worn part may be the real cause. In short, gears manage leverage. They let the rider keep a workable pedaling rhythm across changing terrain by choosing how much force and wheel speed each pedal turn should produce.'''; print(len(re.findall(r\\\"\\\\b[\\\\w\\u2019'-]+\\\\b\\\",s)))\\\"\", \"timeout\": 30}"}, "sequence_number": 11}
